<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-4-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Parameter Space Analysis </title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Predict the effects of inital conditions on trajectories. </p></li>
            <li><p> Predict the effects of boundary conditions on trajectories. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>
    
    <definition xml:id="def-harmonic-motion">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A variable <m>x(t)</m> exhibits <alert>harmonic motion</alert> if it can be described with an equation of the form
                <md>
                    m\ddot{x} + \beta \dot{x} + k \dot{x} = f(t)
                </md>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>f(t) \not\equiv 0 </m>, we call the motion <alert> driven </alert>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>\beta = 0 </m>, we call the motion <alert> free </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>\beta \lt 0</m>, we call the motion <alert> damped </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Free, undriven harmonic motion is called <alert> simple harmonic motion </alert>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

</section>
